<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nitra</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nyitra, Neutra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nitra is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> on the Nitra river, one of the oldest towns in the country. In the early 9th century it was the seat of the Slavic prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pribina">Pribina</a>, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mojmir-i">Mojmír I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> drove him out around 833 and joined it to Great Moravia, and a bishopric was first founded there in 880. It later became a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> under the name Nyitra.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nitra.jpg" alt="Nitra" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ingo Mehling · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nitra_-_St._Emmeram%27s_Cathedral.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nitra lies at 167 m in the valley of the Nitra River in the Danubian Lowland, with part of the city rising at the foot of Zobor hill, 587 m high, in the southernmost reaches of the Tribeč mountains. It is roughly halfway between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Bratislava</a>, 92 km away, and Banská Bystrica, 118 km away. The name derives from the Nitra River.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The oldest finds in Nitra are dated to 25,000 to 30,000 years ago, and the site has been inhabited in all periods of the last 5,000 to 7,000 years. The Madarovce culture built the first fortification on Castle Hill by about 1,600 BCE, and a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> hillfort stood on Zobor. In the Roman period the Germanic Quadi settled the area, and Nitra is mentioned as their possible capital in 396 CE.</p><p>The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> reached what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> at the end of the 5th and early 6th centuries, and the densest Slavic settlement is documented around Nitra. The first mention of the city dates to the 9th century. After Great Moravia broke up, Nitra, unlike other great Moravian centres such as Mikulčice-Valy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pohansko">Pohansko</a>, kept its status as a prestigious centre until the 13th century. Older accounts held that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> destroyed it, but archaeology shows no destruction by fire.</p><p>In the early 14th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthew-iii-csak">Matthew III Csák</a> damaged the town and castle several times, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Nitra stayed loyal to the king, who in 1313 confirmed the bishopric's privileges and extended them to the administration of the whole Nitra County. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the Hungarian National Council in Nitra turned to the Czechoslovak Army to restore order and formally protested on 10 December 1918 against the occupation, but the town became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Nitra has a population of 75,301 and is the seat of the University of Constantinus the Philosopher and the Slovak University of Agriculture. Its sights include Nitra Castle with St. Emmeram's Cathedral, the old town and the Piarist church of St. Ladislaus, remodelled in the baroque style in 1742 to 1748. The oldest church of the city is that of Saint Stephen, built in the 11th and 12th centuries on a 9th-century foundation. The Yeshiva of Nitra, the last yeshiva surviving in occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, moved afterwards to Mount Kisco, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nitra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It is disputed if Pribina was an independent prince of <b>Nitra</b> or Mojmír's lieutenant, but many historians hold that Great Moravia emerged through the enforced union of Nitra with Moravia in the early 830s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/great-moravia">Great Moravia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Pribina, a Slavic prince expelled from <b>Nitra</b> by Mojmir I of Moravia around 833, entered Frankish service.</blockquote><a href="/state/balaton-principality">Balaton Principality</a></li><li><blockquote>It covered two blocks: one around <b>Nyitra</b> (Nitra) in the north-west and one around Bihar in the east.</blockquote><a href="/state/tercia-pars-regni">Tercia pars regni</a></li></ul></section>
